There is little traffic in this group recently, so I decided to tell you
folks the story of me and a bunch of my friends nethacking during our
undergraduate years in U of Toronto.

U of Toronto, like most institutes, has a very restricted network access
for the undergrads.  We only had access to the local machines, and we
did not have a /usr/game directory.  The whole thing started when a guy
showed me how to download the source of nethack from a LOCAL machine.
The next thing we did was to compile the game, put it into a guy's
account, and had the execution permission opened to everyone.  Naturally
we did not want get system administrator's attention.  We rename the
nethack file to 'a.out'.

After nethacking for a while in the computer room, we generate some
attention.  More and more people played the game and got addicted.  The
game became more interesting when people started to pickup artifacts
from the dead bones.  Imagine picking up a +6 Mjollnir and a +5 Strombringer.
Then, A guy wrote a script which screened through the score file and ranked
the players according to their choosen characters.  The game evolved into a
competition.  The players, not satisfied for being mere Demi-gods, were going
after the high scores of each character.

The system administrator eventually found out the existence of the game.
Actually, it didn't take long for him to find out, given that there were
seven or eight people executing 'a.out' for more than 15 minutes.  To our
surprise, he didn't have the game removed.  He gave us a warning not
to played the game when the machine load was high, using the dial-in and
remote port, and 'nice -5' the game.  The game existed in user directories
until the last person who was willing to donate his account to the game
left.

The side effect of intensive nethacking was the players were getting lower
grades than they used to.  But all of us had two years of great fun.  The
moral of the story is, it is much more fun to play nethack with a [large]
group of people than to play it with yourself alone.

